WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:07.149
Pixar brings us Joy, Sadness, Anger, Disgust
and Fear in their 15th animated feature which

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:10.170
takes us inside the mind of 11-year-old Riley.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:16.320
I'm Jan and this is 25 things you probably
didn't know about Inside Out.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.010
The basic design of each of the movie's five
Emotions was inspired by a particular shape:

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:27.660
Joy is based on a star, Sadness on an upside-down
teardrop, Fear on a nerve, Anger on a fire

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.720
brick, and Disgust on broccoli, though director
Pete Docter says he actually loves broccoli!

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.390
When they were animating Joy, the filmmakers
channelled a mix of Audrey Hepburn, Am�lie

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.730
star Audrey Tautou, and Bugs Bunny!

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.220
And the way Joy moves was inspired by gymnasts.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:48.190
For Sadness, the animators took cues from
characters such as Wednesday Addams from The

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.559
Addams Family and Debbie Downer from Saturday
Night Live.

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:56.780
While for Disgust, they were inspired by Parks
And Rec's April Ludgate and Veruca Salt from

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.910
Willy Wonka And The Chocolate Factory.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:03.629
And for Fear, they referenced comedic actor
Don Knotts and Mr Bean.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:08.610
When it came to Anger, even in their early
pitches, the filmmakers had in mind comedian

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.900
Lewis Black, who actually provides the voice
for Anger in the final movie.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:19.030
Kaitlyn Dias, who plays 11-year-old Riley,
was originally just hired to record a temporary

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.530
voice track so the filmmakers could present
their storyboard ideas.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.979
But they loved her performance so much that
they cast her in the final movie.

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:32.720
Not only was the story of Inside Out inspired
by director Pete Docter's experience of his

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.491
daughter, Elie, growing up, but according
to character art director Albert Lozano, Riley

00:01:37.491 --> 00:01:38.780
even looks a lot like Elie!

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:45.939
Oh, and by the way, Elie Docter was also the
voice of young Ellie in Pixar's movie Up.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:50.560
Remember the scenes where Riley makes it quite
clear she's not a fan of a particular green

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.560
vegetable?

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.880
Well, in Japan, kids generally like broccoli,
so the filmmakers re-animated those scenes

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.810
and replaced broccoli with green peppers.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:04.560
And while North American audiences watching
Inside Out see Riley's dad daydreaming about

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.299
ice hockey during dinner, in other places
such as the UK, ice hockey was replaced with

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:11.590
soccer.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:16.510
Riley's imaginary friend, Bing Bong, was partly
inspired by Pete Docter's own imaginary friend

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:20.579
from childhood, a car-driving little elephant
called Norman!

00:02:20.579 --> 00:02:25.590
The animation team also channelled comic actors
Oliver Hardy, Jackie Gleason, and John Candy

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.040
for Bing Bong.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.739
For the filmmakers, it was essential to give
both the Human world and the world inside

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:36.409
Riley's mind a distinct look so audiences
could immediately tell the difference between

00:02:36.409 --> 00:02:37.409
them.

00:02:37.409 --> 00:02:42.569
So, the artists looked to 1950s Broadway musicals
for the bright, saturated colours of the Mind

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:47.230
World, and to give the Human World a more
real feel, there's more of a handheld or Steadicam

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.269
look.

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:53.719
To come up with the design of the Long-Term
Memory storage area, the filmmakers researched

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:55.360
various manufacturing facilities.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.319
They took a tour of the Jelly Belly candy
factory, where they were inspired by the way

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:04.040
the colours moved through the tubes in their
automated packaging machinery.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:09.069
And they also visited an egg processing plant
to get ideas for how delicate objects like

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:13.299
memories could be moved from one place to
another.

00:03:13.299 --> 00:03:17.359
Pete Docter has described Dream Productions,
the place where Riley's dreams and nightmares

00:03:17.359 --> 00:03:21.129
are created, as a mix of Saturday Night Live
and the magic of Hollywood!

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:25.620
In fact, as inspiration for the movie, Docter
spent a week observing behind the scenes of

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:32.439
Saturday Night Live, as a guest of Bill Hader,
who plays the voice of Fear in Inside Out.

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:37.420
Director Pete Docter and producer Jonas Rivera
are both massive fans of Disneyland, so when

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:42.439
it came to creating the right look for Imagination
Land in Riley's Mind World, theme parks were

00:03:42.439 --> 00:03:45.790
their biggest inspiration.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:50.260
When they were designing the Subconscious,
Inside Out's artists drew heavily on classic

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:55.269
horror movies, especially for their theatrical
lighting.

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:59.639
To develop the look of the Train Of Thought,
which delivers daydreams and ideas in the

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:04.709
Mind World, the filmmakers visited a train
museum and also consulted John Lasseter, who's

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:10.099
not only Chief Creative Officer at Walt Disney
and Pixar Animation Studios, but also a life-long

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:13.400
train enthusiast!

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.650
Just like Riley, Pete Docter grew up in Minnesota
and later moved to the Bay Area, though according

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:24.039
to him, in Inside Out, moving is more of a
metaphor for growing up.

00:04:24.039 --> 00:04:29.210
The address of Riley's home in San Francisco
is 21 Royal Street, which just happens to

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:31.760
be the name of a new private dining venue
at Disneyland.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.970
And as a nod to the Walt Disney Family Museum,
the filmmakers located Riley's hockey rink

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:41.930
in the same place the museum is located in
San Francisco.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:46.569
When composer Michael Giacchino first discussed
the movie's score at a construction site where

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:50.569
Pete Docter was building a house, little did
he know the location of that meeting would

00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:52.050
influence the movie's music.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:56.130
But some pieces of metal that Giacchino's
son found at the site produced such a nice

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:00.060
sound when hit that Giacchino gave them to
the percussion section to play with little

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.110
metal tongs.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:07.150
You can hear them in the first musical cue,
just after the memory ball comes down.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:11.580
To make sure they really understood how the
mind works and also how a pre-teen might deal

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.560
with traumatic events, Inside Out's filmmakers
worked with scientists, neurologists, and

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.030
psychologists.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:22.520
And Dacher Keltner, who's professor of psychology
at the University of California, Berkeley,

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.960
helped them choose which emotions to feature
in the film out of the more than 20 emotions

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.580
recognised by psychologists.

